From: "John M. Bozeman" <jmb5b@faraday.clas.virginia.edu>
Date: Sat, 3 Dec 1994 03:26:27 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-99] BASICS: Environment and markets

Forgive me of my environmental skepticism, but...  I find it hard
to believe that tourism revenues could successfully compete
with mining and drilling.  A car of people into a park will yield--
what?  $5 for a day visit, and _maybe_ $15/day (or less) to camp?
On the other hand, a barrel of oil will go for something like
$25-30 each.  Copper (which I believe is strip-mined) goes
for $1.36/lb, mercury serveral dollars a pound, tin $4/lb, etc.

While I realize that there are expenses in mining, and that
there is a variable amount of ore processed per lb of 
product, I find it hard to believe that parks could 
compete economically with a nice, cheap strip mine
, with the cheapest methods available.

BTW, I would also like to understand why a laissez-faire
economy would be under any obligation to use any sort
of pollution safe-guards...  Even with our present "over
regulated" economy there is the problem of the fly-by-
night waste disposal company... it goes something like
this:

Fairly reputable company A generates toxic waste, say,
cynide solutions.  They pay Fly By Night Inc. to take
the waste.  They do; A is now clear.  BUT!  FBN Inc. 
simply stores the waste on-site in drums somewhere...
when they run out of drums, they abandon the site,
and the drums start to leak into the water table,etc.
Since FBN is a corp., there is not really anybody
left to sue, and the gov't gets stuck trying to clean
it up.  Of course, we _could_ just let it sit...
if we like to drink bottled water, and try not
to swallow the water in the shower, and ignore that
funny hydrocarbon smell each time we flush...

Not to mention the fact that FBN would have set up
shop on the cheapest land possible, probably in a low-income
region...

I am not trying to be either paranoid or a bleeding
heart; I simply used to work in the field of environmental
engineering, where the question was how to get rid of the 
most waste for minimal money, w/o breaking to rules
or simply dumping it on some poor hapless schmuck
who wasn't as up on loopholes as we happened to be...

