From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Sat, 3 Dec 1994 10:58:59 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-104] Singularity: Bah! Humbug!

Jordan Sparks <sparksj@ucs.orst.edu> sez:

>This is a bunch of garbage.

I agree completely.

Yes, Perry (et al), *assuming* that uploads are at all possible (a very big
assumption, in my opinion), we might be able to think 1,000,000 times
faster than we can now.  So what?  What's the difference if we can say
"Hello" in one second or in a microsecond?  All that wil do is change our
basic perceptual timescale from secs to microsecs (or whatever); it won't
change the physics at all.

Not until we can think and act on a scale which is comparable to Planck's
constant (6.63E-34 J-sec) will there be any non-linear change in our
perceived universe, and before we reach that point there will be *lots* of
other difficulties to surpass.  Mass will still be mass, energy will still
be energy, and all the stuff that's difficult for us now will still be
difficult for us then.  And please don't bring into the discussion that
holy panacea of Nanotech: just call me when it happens.

It's my understanding that the so-called "singularity" will appear so only
to those who do not avail themselves of advancing technology.  There are
civilizations on this planet which have not changed much in the past 1,000+
years, while we in the West have marched on with technology.  I doubt that
they view us as any sort of "singularity", or that they really care much
about our technological advances at all (except as a product line).  The
preoccupation with technology does seems to be primarily a Western cultural
abberation.

In any case, I thought that the singularity in "Marooned in Realtime" was
supposed to represent either (a) everyone going off into space, (b)
everyone bobbling themselves into the future, or (c) some natural disaster.
I think it's far more likely that the planet will be (e.g) hit by a comet
in the next 100 years (effectively ending this discussion) than that there
will be some kind of techno-mystical event which elevates us all to
godhood.

Discussion...?

--Vince

