From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Sat, 3 Dec 1994 21:14:17 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-106] Caught in the Machinary of Friedman

Fred Hapgood said:

      >Friedman seems to think [...] people won't be willing
      >to pay to restrain the behavior of others.  We must live on
      >different planets.

John Clark riposted:

      People won't be willing to pay to restrain the private
      behavior of others in most areas, child abuse and perhaps a
      few others excepted.

Like I say, we must live on different planets.  In my home
country -- I doubt you would have heard of it -- private groups
can raise millions in donations on the promise that they will do
precisely that.  We have groups that try to restrain the behavior
of pregnant women, gays, loggers, hunters, whalers, porn lovers,
gun owners, property developers ...  but I see you don't believe
me.  Oh well.

Moving on: Let's consider how a security service on the Friedman model
would work wrt personal and home security, which is perhaps the
simplest case. (Simpler, for instance, than figuring out how a private
service would protect your investments and enforce your contracts.)

The obvious place to begin is with the menu of services already
offered by private security firms.  In addition to these the
private security firms would handle the disposition of criminal
cases -- they would try the perps if there were trials; imprison
them if there were prisons; execute them if there was capital
punishment.

Services would bid for our business by promising performance not
just on patrolling activity and response times, which is how we
judge private security services now, but on how suspects were
treated: what sort of trials they would get and what sort of
punishments they would receive if guilty, etc.

As a rule I would imagine people would be most interested in the
service promising the most economical procedures all along the
line, to wit, a quick sidewalk interrogation and then either a
memorable beating or a bullet to the brain, depending on how the
cost/benefit analysis broke.  Such a service would be able to
underbid dramatically any agency that promised such frills as
full trials, imprisonment, an appeals process, and the like.  I
have to say that at least in my neighborhood, which is not rich,
a fair number of people would feel they just couldn't afford
anything more lavish than the bare-bones model.  (Some people
would be willing to pay *more* for the model with the summary
execution provisions.)

Friedman might say that such a service wouldn't in fact be
offered, because perps and suspects would have contracted with
their own agencies for protection, and those agencies would
insist on their clients getting something like the spectrum of
protections we associate with the contemporary justice system.

I dunno.  Suppose they *hadn't* so contracted?  Suppose they had
missed the last payment?  Suppose they were gambling that they
could get away without paying the monthly fee to their agency,
like people running without health insurance? Suppose they had an
agency, but it was a cheapie contract that promised protection
only in the north side of town, and here they were in the south
side?

Bang, right?  
